{% block title %}The Trespass By-law session was backwards | RHT Circle{% endblock %}
{% block description %}The July 28 recording shows how Sagamok's Trespass By-law draft began, what powers it creates and which questions only leadership could answer.{% endblock %}
{% block og_title %}The Trespass By-law session was backwards{% endblock %}
{% block og_description %}The lawyers could explain the draft. They could not answer what Council intends to do with it.{% endblock %}

{% block article_body %}
  <p class="lead">Two lawyers explained a by-law Council asked them to draft. The people responsible for asking for it, deciding what evidence supports it and deciding whether to pass it did not sit at the front and answer members.</p>

  <p>That mattered because the lawyers could explain what the draft says. They could not explain what Council intends to do with it.</p>

  <div class="news-article__plain">
    <strong>The problem with the session</strong>
    <p>The session had the people who wrote the by-law. It did not have the people who must answer for it.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>What we learned</h2>

  <p>The lawyer said Sagamok contacted the firm because existing banishment orders were not being enforced. He said Council and Community Justice asked for a law that police and the courts could enforce.</p>

  <p>The firm was told the Residency By-law was not being used. Instead of fixing it, the firm drafted a new Trespass By-law.</p>

  <p>This happened before the community was brought into the process.</p>

  <h2>What the draft does</h2>

  <p>The draft starts by treating a non-member on Sagamok territory as a trespasser until they prove otherwise.</p>

  <p>Chief and Council or an enforcement officer could identify and remove someone. An officer could demand identification, issue a notice, seize items, arrest someone without a warrant and remove them from the territory.</p>

  <p>Refusing an order or refusing to identify yourself would become separate offences. Council could appoint the enforcement officers and appoint the Appeal Committee. Council could also stop services to someone who had been trespassed.</p>

  <p>More than 20 existing banishment orders would remain in effect.</p>

  <h2>The safeguards are not ready</h2>

  <p>The Appeal Committee has not been designed. The rules for appointing officers have not been written. The appeal process, information-sharing rules and enforcement plan have not been written. Housing Policy would be changed to support evictions.</p>

  <p>The lawyers said this work could happen after Council passes the by-law and before it comes into force.</p>

  <div class="news-article__plain">
    <strong>Power first, safeguards later</strong>
    <p>Council would approve the power first. Much of the protection against misuse would come later.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>What the lawyers could not answer</h2>

  <p>Members asked whether Council intends to approve this draft.</p>

  <blockquote class="news-article__quote">
    “That's up to your Council. We draft things at the request of the Council. We don't make the decision.”
  </blockquote>

  <p>Members asked whether the Membership Law would be completed first.</p>

  <blockquote class="news-article__quote">
    “I don't have that answer.”
  </blockquote>

  <p>Members asked whether there would be more engagement. The lawyer said he had been told there would be more sessions, but no dates had been arranged.</p>

  <p>They could not tell members what evidence Council used, how many incidents involved gangs, drugs or outsiders, whether APS has agreed to enforce the by-law, who will sit on an independent Appeal Committee, how the session feedback will change the draft, whether members will see the policies before Council votes or when Council plans to make its decision.</p>

  <p>Those are Council questions. Council was not answering them.</p>

  <h2>The draft and the presentation did not match</h2>

  <p>The presentation defined Sagamok territory as lands within the Robinson-Huron Treaty area traditionally used and occupied by Sagamok.</p>

  <p>When a member asked about that definition, the lawyer said the by-law would apply to reserve land only.</p>

  <p>Those are not the same thing. The draft needs one clear boundary and a map showing exactly where these powers would apply.</p>

  <h2>Non-member spouses are still not protected clearly</h2>

  <p>A member asked what happens when a member's non-member spouse hunts, fishes, cuts wood or participates in Fall Harvest.</p>

  <p>The answer was that a family or community invitation would provide authority. The draft does not provide a clear process for proving that invitation. It does not explain what happens when an officer does not believe the person. It does not explain how a spouse already living in Sagamok proves they have permission before being treated as a trespasser.</p>

  <h2>Council would oversee itself</h2>

  <p>The lawyers agreed that an appeal should not be politically tied to Council. The draft still allows Council to appoint the Appeal Committee. There is no independent committee in place.</p>

  <p>One member said that was why the draft should not have been written yet.</p>

  <p>That is the central problem. Community engagement was used to react to a finished legal draft. It should have been used to decide what Sagamok needs before the draft was written.</p>

  <h2>What members put on the record</h2>

  <p>Members did not say community safety was unimportant. They asked whether this is the least harmful way to protect it.</p>

  <p>They raised family separation, non-member spouses, loss of housing and services, access to homes and property, forced identification, surveillance, use of force, political control of appeals, restorative justice, addiction treatment, membership and belonging, and the harm caused when a removal decision is wrong.</p>

  <p>The lawyers could discuss legal wording. They could not decide how Sagamok should answer those questions.</p>

  <section class="news-article__questions">
    <h2>What needs to happen before a vote</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Release the Council direction that started this work.</li>
      <li>Release the incident numbers and evidence used to justify it.</li>
      <li>Provide the written APS enforcement plan.</li>
      <li>Show the full cost of officers, prosecutions and appeals.</li>
      <li>Release the Appeal Committee rules and conflict protections.</li>
      <li>Release the information-sharing and privacy rules.</li>
      <li>Publish the exact territory map.</li>
      <li>Set clear rules for spouses, residents, workers, visitors and vendors.</li>
      <li>Independently review the 20-plus existing banishment orders.</li>
      <li>Release a revised draft showing what changed because of community input.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <p>The Community Justice representative said he was there as a technician and adviser and could not speak for Council.</p>

  <p>That was the clearest answer of the night.</p>
